<?xml version='1.0' ?>
<rrx namespace="ExportToText" v="9.1.6">
  <com ref="Datacap.Libraries.ExportToText.Actions"  qi=" : Creates a plain text data file using application data">
    <h>
      The ExportToText action library provides actions that will export field values, DCO variables,
      batch information and other text to a plain text export file.  The export file can be in a UTF-8 or UTF-16
      format.<br/><br/>

      The application can control the name of the export file and its location.  If the export file is written
      to a shared folder, then the export file name should be unique using the batch ID or other identifier that
      is guaranteed to be unique so one batch does not overwrite the export file from a different batch.<br/><br/>

      Once created, the file can be moved or sent to an external repository using subsequent Datacap actions.
      Alternatively, the export file can be saved in a directory that will be picked up by an external process.
      If an external process is picking up the exported file, it is up to the application designer to ensure that
      the external process does not try to pick up the file prior to it being completely written out to disk.<br/><br/>

      An application can create any number of export files. There can be multiple plain text files created with this
      action library or other export files can be created in addition to the plain text file created by this action library.<br/><br/>

      The format of the file and the data to include in the file is up to the application designer.  The text can be lines without
      any specific length, the file can be created as a comma separated file, fixed length data, or any combination of these
      types.<br/><br/>

      The approach to creating a text file is to first identify the name and location of the file. This information can
      be provided dynamically at runtime, allowing for a file to be named with the batch ID, date stamp, time stamp, etc.
      The file information is typically provided on a batch open, but can be provided at any level.<br/><br/>

      Once the file name and location has been provided, the actions can then be called to export data as needed.  Data can be
      exported from any type of object: batch, document, page or field, but specific actions may only support being called
      on certain types of objects.<br/><br/>

      A typical operation would be to export data at field level. By using export actions on the page level node to export
      the field data, or actions on the field level, as the rules engine iterates through each Datacap object within
      the application, the plain text file can be built, piece by piece. For example, if CSV output is enabled, each field
      can be output and as each field is placed into the output file, the actions will automatically add commas between
      each exported field, with an end result of all of the fields in a line of text, separated by commas, or any desired
      separator character. At the last field, the action StartNewLine can be called to indicate that when the next
      action is called to write to the file, it will be placed on a new line.  If there are multiple pages being exported
      into the file, then it will have the result of placing the field data for page 1 on the first line, the field data
      for page 2 on the next line, the field data for page 3 on the next line, etc. The placement of the actions and the
      DCO nodes they are attached to will determine the format of the output file.<br/><br/>

      Even if an export action is configured to export a field, the export of that field can be configured
      to be skipped at runtime based on the value of the DCO variable NOEXPORT or the status of the field.
      Any fields that have a DCO variable called "NOEXPORT" set to the value of "1" will be
      excluded from the export file, regardless of the field's status.<br/><br/>

      Once all of the data has been written, then the file needs to be closed with the CloseExportFile action.  This
      is typically called on the batch level close of the ruleset that performed the open, or could be at the batch level in
      a subsequent ruleset.  While actions can be split across multiple rulesets, when generating a file,
      all of the actions called to create the file must be within the same task profile if the data
      is to be included in the same export file.<br/><br/>

      The following is a simple ruleset to provide an overview of how the export actions can be used
      within an application.  Assuming that each page has three fields called Field1, Field2 and Field3,
      this example will first create an export file named based on the current batch id, then export
      the date at the top of the export file.  The rest of the file will contain three
      comma separated entries for each page. ExportText("@X") will export the text
      value for each field.  Once all of the fields of all of the pages are exported, the export
      file will be closed.<br/><br/>

      BatchOpenRule<br/>
      - Function<br/>
      - - SetExportDirectory("@APPPATH(export)")<br/>
      - - SetExportFileName("@BATCHID+.txt")<br/>
      - - ExportText("Current Date: +@DATE()")<br/>
      - - SetCSVMode("On")<br/><br/>

      PageOpenRule<br/>
      - Function<br/>
      - - StartNewLine()<br/><br/>

      Field1Rule<br/>
      - Function<br/>
      - - ExportText("@X")<br/><br/>

      Field2Rule<br/>
      - Function<br/>
      - - ExportText("@X")<br/><br/>

      Field3Rule<br/>
      - Function<br/>
      - - ExportText("@X")<br/><br/>

      BatchCloseRule<br/>
      - Function<br/>
      - - CloseExportFile()<br/><br/>

      If the fields contained names, the sample output file could look like this:<br/><br/>

      Current Date: 02/10/2025<br/>
      Mary,Patricia,Linda<br/>
      Barbara,Elizabeth,Jennifer<br/>
      Maria,Susan,Margaret<br/><br/><br/>      


      It is recommended that the application developer understands how the Datacap object hierarchy, DCO, operates.
      Information about the DCO can be found in the help for the ApplicationObjects action library.<br/><br/>

      In addition to the data processed within the batch, it is likely desired that other data also be included
      in the export file such as the batch ID, date, time, job ID, and other environmental information
      be saved within the export file. These values can be obtained using smart parameter notation
      as input to actions.  Refer to the smart parameter guide for more information on using these
      types of parameters.<br/><br/>
    </h>

    <method name="CloseExportFile" qi="Closes the currently opened export file.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <h>
        Closes the currently opened Export file.
        Once closed, it would be expected that if further calls are made to export data, that
        a new file name is provided to the action library usin ghe SetExportFileName action.
        If a subsequent export action is called to write to the file and the file name
        has not been changed, the existing file will be overwritten with the new data.<br/><br/>

        This action usually belongs to its own batch level rule.  However, it can be used at any level.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>CloseExportFile()</b><br/>
          In a typical application, this action will be placed on a batch close event, with the SetExportFileName and
          SetExportDirectory actions called on a batch open event. This action will close the file.
          <br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <see>
        SetExportFileName, SetExportDirectory<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="ExportAllPageFields" qi="Exports all field values on the current page, including values of Line Item Detail sub-fields - with exceptions.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <h>
        This action exports all field values of the current page. The value of each field is saved
        in the export file on the same line.  If CSV mode is enabled, then each field will be separated
        by the configured separator character.<br/><br/>
        
        The action does not
        export those values of field objects of the Document Hierarchy with:<br/><br/>
        
        1. The DCO  variable NOEXPORT with a value of "1".<br/><br/>

        2. If the current status of the field matches the status configured with the action SetIgnoreFieldStatus.<br/><br/>      
        
        <e>
          <b>ExportAllFields()</b><br/>
          This action must be called on a page level DCO object.  Each of the page's child fields 
          will have its text value placed into the export file.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Page level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the action is not used at the Page level. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="ExportBlankCSVFields" qi="Inserts blank fields into the Export file, adjacent to the current field.">
      <p name="Count"/>
      <ap>
        <b>Count</b> A number indicating how many blank fields to add to the Export file.
      </ap>
      <h>
        The <b>ExportBlankCSVFields</b> action adds the number of fields you specify. This action
        only has an effect if CSV mode is enabled.  It will create blank entries within the CSV file.
        If this action is called on a field object, the text value of the field does not matter as only
        blank entries will be created in the export file.<br/><br/>

        Note: Make sure you call SetCSVMode, and optionally SetCSVElementSeparator, to set the separator values as
        desired for your export file.  If neither of these actions are called before
        this action, no empty fields will be exported because 
        the default separator is set to no separator.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          SetCSVMode("On")<br/>
          <b>ExportBlankCSVFields("12")</b><br/><br/>

          This example will add 12 blank entries in the CSV file, such as ",,,,,,,,,,,".
          Additionally, this example shows the action SetCSVMode called directly before the blank fields action.
          This is for illustration.  As long as the action is run prior to calling ExportBlankCSVFields, on any DCO
          object, then the mode will be in effect for subsequent actions.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All levels.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        SetSCVMode<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="ExportCurrentField" qi="Exports the current field value to the Export file.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <h>
        Exports the current field value to the Export file.  The exported value will
        be on the same line as the previous exported value, unless StartNewLine
        has been called. If CSV mode has been enabled, then the value will be
        preceded by the current CSV separator value.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>ExportCurrentField()</b><br/><br/>
          
          This example would be called on a field object. The current text value of the field
          will be added to the export file.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Field level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        False if there is an error, otherwise <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="ExportFillerCharacters" qi="Adds a string of filler characters to the Export.">      
      <p name="Count"/>
      <p name="CharactersToExport"/>
      <ap>
        <b>Count</b> A number indicating the total length in characters of the filler.<br/><br/>
        
        <b>CharacterToExport:</b> One or more characters to place into the export file.
        This value can be a single character or multiple characters.  If multiple characters
        are provided, then the sequence will be repeated based on the provided count.<br/><br/>        
        
        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.
      </ap>
      <h>
        Adds a single or multiple filler characters to the export file, repeated by the number of times indicated in the
        "Count" input parameter.<br/><br/>
        
        If called on a field object, the filler string is written to the file regardless of the data in the current field.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>ExportFillerCharacters("4",":")</b><br/>          
          The action in the example fills the current field with 4 instances of the character ":" such as
          "::::" to the export file.<br/><br/>

          <b>ExportFillerCharacters("4","@B.MyVariable")</b><br/>
          Similar to the previous example, however this obtains the filler text from
          the DCO variable called "MyVariable" which is expected to be on the batch level DCO.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All levels.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the first parameter is not numeric or if there is an error.
        Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="ExportPageField" qi="Exports the specified Field object's value to the Export file.">
      <p name="FieldToExport"/>
      <ap>
        <b>FieldToExport:</b> The name of the child Field object whose value you want to export.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Exports the specified Field object's value to the Export file. Will only export the
        first Field if multiple fields of the same field type are found.<br/><br/>

        The text will be exported on the same line as the previously exported text
        unless StartNewLine was previously called.  If CSV mode is enabled, then the data
        will be exported with the configured separator character.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>ExportFieldValue("Date")</b><br/>
          <b>ExportFieldValue("Number")</b><br/>
          <b>ExportFieldValue("Total")</b><br/><br/>

          This sequence exports the current values stored in the Date, Number and Total fields.
          These fields must be children of the current page.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Page level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the parameter is not a Field object's name or if an error occurs. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>
    
    <method name="ExportPageFieldFixedLengthLeftJustified" qi="Exports a specified number of characters from a field's left end (left-justified.)">
      <p name="FieldToExport"/>
      <p name="FixedLength"/>
      <ap>        
        <b>FieldToExport:</b> The field's name to export.  This is the name of the corresponding field object of the Document Hierarchy.
        the field must be a child object of the current page.<br/><br/>
        
        <b>FixedLength:</b> All exported values will be adjusted to this length.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action exports the text value of the named field to the export file.  The field
        must be a child of the current page.  The field will be right justified and adjusted to
        the length provided.  If the value is longer, it will be truncated.
        If the field is shorter than the provided length, then
        the exported value will be padded to the end using the current pad character
        to make the value the exact length specified.<br/><br/>

        Calling this action will enable left justified, fixed length export for all subsequent
        export actions.<br/><br/>        
        <e>
          <b>ExportPageFieldFixedLengthLeftJustified("Volume","8")</b><br/>
          This example assumes the current page has a field called "Volume". The text
          value of that field will be added to the export file and adjusted to be 8 characters
          long.  If the text is shorter than 8 characters, it will be padded using the current
          fill character.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Page level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if either parameter is invalid or if the action is called at the wrong level.
        Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        ExportPageFieldFixedLengthRightJustified, SetFillCharacter<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="ExportPageFieldFixedLengthRightJustified" qi="Exports a specified number of characters from a field's right end (right-justified.)">
      <p name="FieldToExport"/>
      <p name="FixedLength"/>
      <ap>
        <b>FieldToExport:</b> The field's name to export.  This is the name of the corresponding field object of the Document Hierarchy.
        the field must be a child object of the current page.<br/><br/>

        <b>FixedLength:</b> All exported values will be adjusted to this length.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.
      </ap>
      <lvl>Page level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if either parameter is invalid or if the action is called at the wrong level.
        Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <h>
        This action exports the text value of the named field to the export file.  The field
        must be a child of the current page.  The field will be right justified and adjusted to
        the length provided.  If the value is longer, it will be truncated.        
        If the field is shorter than the provided length, then
        the exported value will be padded from the front using the current pad character
        to make the value the exact length specified.<br/><br/>        

        Calling this action will enable right justified, fixed length export for all subsequent
        export actions.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>ExportPageFieldFixedLengthRightJustified("Volume","8")</b><br/>
          This example assumes the current page has a field called "Volume". The text
          value of that field will be added to the export file and adjusted to be 8 characters
          long.  If the text is shorter than 8 characters, it will be padded using the current
          fill character.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <see>        
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="ExportText" qi="Exports a value to the Export file.">
      <p name="Text"/>
      <ap>
        <b>Text:</b> The value to export.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <h>
        The provided text will be exported to the export file.  It will be added to the same
        line as the previous exported value unless the action StartNewLine has first
        been called.  If CSV mode or left / right justification has been enabled,
        then this value will also be adjusted based on the settings prior to writing
        to the text file.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported, providing a wide range of additional values
        and locations from which data can be obtained for writing to the export file.<br/><br/>

        This action is similar to the action ExportTextNotJustified, the difference is
        that this action will respect the current left or right justification
        mode, if it is enabled.<br/><br/>

        <e>        
          <b>ExportText("Hello There")</b><br/>
          This example will write the text "Hello There" to the export file.  The value will
          be exported as CSV if the CSV mode is enabled. The value will also be adjusted
          to be left or right justified, if justification mode has been enabled.<br/><br/>
          
          <b>ExportText("@DATE")</b><br/>
          This example uses a smart parameter to place the current date into the
          export file.<br/><br/>

          <b>ExportText("@BATCHID")</b><br/>
          This example uses a smart parameter to place the current batch ID into the
          export file.<br/><br/>

          <b>ExportText("Current Operator: +@OPERATOR")</b><br/>
          If the ID of the current operator is "Tom", then this will output the
          text "Current Operator: Tom" in the export file.<br/><br/>

          <b>ExportText("@P\MyField.MyVariable"</b><br/>
          Assuming this is called on a page or field DCO, the smart parameter will navigate from the page
          to the field called "MyField", obtain the value of the variable "MyVariable"
          and write that value to the export file.<br/><br/>

          <b>ExportText("@P\MyField")</b><br/>
          This example is similar to the previous example, but it will instead
          obtain the text value of the field "MyField" and save it in the export file.<br/><br/>

          <b>ExportTExt("@PILOT(BATCHDIR)")</b><br/><br/>
          This example will place the path to the current batch into the exported file.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        NotJustified, SetSCVMode, SetCSVElementSeparator<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="ExportTextNotJustified" qi="Exports an evaluated smart parameter value to the Export file.">
      <p name="Text"/>
      <ap>
        <b>Text:</b> The value to export.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <h>
        The provided text will be exported to the export file.  It will be added to the same
        line as the previous exported value unless the action StartNewLine has first
        been called.  If CSV mode or left / right justification has been enabled,
        it will have no effect on the output value.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported, providing a wide range of additional values
        and locations from which data can be obtained for writing to the export file.<br/><br/>

        This action is similar to the action ExportText, the difference is
        that this action will not justify the output value even if
        the left or right justification
        mode is enabled.<br/><br/>

        <e>
          <b>ExportTextNotJustified("Hello There")</b><br/>
          This example will write the text "Hello There" to the export file.  The value will
          be exported as CSV if the CSV mode is enabled. The value will also be adjusted
          to be left or right justified, if justification mode has been enabled.<br/><br/>

          <b>ExportTextNotJustified("@DATE")</b><br/>
          This example uses a smart parameter to place the current date into the
          export file.<br/><br/>

          <b>ExportTextNotJustified("@BATCHID")</b><br/>
          This example uses a smart parameter to place the current batch ID into the
          export file.<br/><br/>

          <b>ExportTextNotJustified("Current Operator: +@OPERATOR")</b><br/>
          If the ID of the current operator is "Tom", then this will output the
          text "Current Operator: Tom" in the export file.<br/><br/>

          <b>ExportTextNotJustified("@P\MyField.MyVariable"</b><br/>
          Assuming this is called on a page or field DCO, the smart parameter will navigate from the page
          to the field called "MyField", obtain the value of the variable "MyVariable"
          and write that value to the export file.<br/><br/>

          <b>ExportTextNotJustified("@P\MyField")</b><br/>
          This example is similar to the previous example, but it will instead
          obtain the text value of the field "MyField" and save it in the export file.<br/><br/>

          <b>ExportTExtNotJustified("@PILOT(BATCHDIR)")</b><br/><br/>
          This example will place the path to the current batch into the exported file.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        ExportText<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="LineItemAddElement" qi="Includes the specified Line Item Field object as an element of a Line Item Array. ">
      <p name="FieldName"/>
      <ap>
        <b>FieldName:</b> The name of the child field object of the Document Hierarchy to export.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action is used for exporting Line Item values.
        This action includes the specified Line Item field object as an element of a Line Item Array.<br/><br/>

        The elements added to the line item elements are either names of fields to export, blank
        export entries, or smart parameters that will be evaluated.
        When LineItemExportElements is called, it will process each of the line item "elements" that have
        been added. The items added by LineItemAddElement are names of line item fields that will be exported.<br/><br/>

        A Line Item Array accumulates and organizes captured line item values retrieved from the Data
        file of a particular page.<br/><br/>

        <b>Note:</b> A rule using this action should be applied to the <i>LINEITEM</i> fields
        of the Document Hierarchy.<br/><br/>        
        <e>
          <b>LineItemAddElement("Price")</b><br/>
          LineItemExportElements()<br/><br/>

          The action above expands the Line Item Array by one field: Price.<br/><br/>

          LineItemExportElements will populate this element and other elements
          of the array with the captured values it finds in a page's Data file before exporting them.
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>
        The parent field that contains the child Line Item field.
      </lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        LineItemExportElements,  LineItemClearElements, LineItemAddBlankFields<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="LineItemAddBlankFields" qi="Includes the specified number of blank fields as elements of a Line Item Array. ">
      <p name="Count"/>
      <ap>
        <b>Count:</b> The number of Blank CSV field values to export as part of the Line Item Array.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action includes the specified number of Blank Line Item fields as elements
        of a Line Item Array.<br/><br/>

        A Line Item Array accumulates and organizes captured line item values retrieved
        from the Data file of a particular page.  This action will add "blank" values
        to the collection that will be exported when LineItemExportElements is called.<br/><br/>

        <b>Note:</b> A rule using this action must be applied to the <i>LINEITEM</i> fields of the Document Hierarchy.<br/><br/>

        This action is used for exporting Blank Line Item values.
        <e>
          <b>LineItemAddBlankFields("6")</b><br/><br/>

          The action above expands the Line Item Array by six blank fields<br/><br/>

          LineItemExportElements will populate this element and other elements
          of the array with the captured values it finds in a page's Data file before exporting them.
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>
        The parent field that contains the child Line Item field.
      </lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        LineItemExportElements, LineItemClearElements,LineItemAddElements, LineItemAddSmartParameter<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="LineItemAddSmartParameter" qi="Add a smart parameter algorithm as an element of a Line Item Array. ">
      <p name="SmartParameter"/>
      <ap>
        <b>SmartParameter:</b> Smart Parameter to be evaluated during processing of the Lineitem array.
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action permits adding a smart parameter as an element of a Line Item Array to be evaluated
        during the Array processing.<br/><br/>

        A Line Item Array accumulates and organizes captured line item values retrieved
        from the Data file of a particular page.<br/><br/>
        
        The elements added to the line item elements are either names of line item 
        fields to export, blank export entries, or
        smart parameters that will be evaluated.
        When LineItemExportElements is called, it will process each of the line item "elements" that have
        been added. The "Smart Parameter" elements that have been added will be evaluated as the elements
        are processed.

        <b>Important!</b> A rule using this action must be applied to the <i>LINEITEM</i> fields of the Document Hierarchy.<br/><br/>        
        <e>
          <b>LineItemAddSmartParameter("@F\Price+@TIME(HH:MM:SS)")</b><br/>
          LineItemExportElements()<br/><br/>

          The action above places a child field Price of the calling field node (@F)
          appended with the current time in format HH:MM:SS to the export file. <br/><br/>

          LineItemExportElements will populate this element and other elements
          of the array with the captured values it finds in a page's Data file before exporting them.
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>
        The parent field that contains the child Line Item field.
      </lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        LineItemExportElements, LineItemClearElements, LineItemAddElements, LineItemAddBlankFields<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.<br/>
        Refer to the smart parameter guide for more information on available smart parameters.
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="LineItemClearElements" qi="Clears values in the Line Item Array.">
      <ap>None</ap>
      <h>
        This is mainly a housekeeping function you can use to be sure the array does not contain leftover values.
        <e>
          <b>LineItemClearElements()</b><br/>
          This example will clear out any previously set line item elements for export.
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>
        The parent Field object of the Document Hierarchy that contains a child
        Line Item field, such as you may typically find in an invoice application.
      </lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        LineItemAddElement, LineItemAddExportElements, LineItemAddBlankFields, LineItemAddSmartParameter<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>
    

    <method name="LineItemExportElements" qi="Exports the captured values in a page's Line Item Array that have been populated with LineItemAddElement actions.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <lvl>
        The parent field that contains the child Line Item sub-fields.
      </lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <h>
        Exports the captured values in a page's Line Item Array that have been populated
        with LineItemAddElement actions.
        <e>
          LineItemAddElement("Price")<br/>
          LineItemAddElement("LineTotal")<br/>
          <b>LineItemExportElements()</b><br/>
          StartNewLine()<br/><br/>

          This example exports the values included in the Line Item Array to your Export file.
        </e>
      </h>
      <see>
        LineItemClearElements, LineItemAddElement, LineItemAddBlankFields, LineItemAddSmartParameter<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>
    
    
    <method name="ResetFieldExportSettings" qi="Resets the variables of the bound Field object of the Document Hierarchy.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <h>
        This action resets some of the export settings that can be independently set by other actions.
        It is provided as a quick way to reset custom export settings.<br/><br/>
        
        + The default field fill character is reset to a space.<br/>
        + Field justification is reset to left and turned off.<br/>
        + Fields are no longer set to a specific length.<br/>
        + The ignore field status is reset to not ignore any fields based on the status.<br/>
        
        <e>
          <b>ResetFieldExportSettings()</b><br/>
          This action can be called on any level and resets the values mentioned. Alternatively,
          the user could have instead called the individual actions to turn off the features.
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        SetFixedLength, SetJustifiedExportMode, SetFillCharacter and SetIgnoreFieldStatus<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>
    
    <method name="SaveExportFilePathAsVariable" qi="Saves the path and name of your Export file to the variable specified by the parameter. ">
      <p name="VariableName"/>
      <ap>
        <b>VariableName:</b> The name of the variable name to store the full name and path of the export file.
        If the variable does not exist, it will be created.<br/>
        Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Saves the path and name of your Export file to the variable specified by the parameter.  If the
        variable does not exist, it will be created.  The value will be placed into the current DCO
        object and can be accessed by subsequent actions in the same rule, in a following rule or
        in a following task. Unless it is specifically cleared, this value will exist for
        the life of the batch.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>SaveExportFilePathAsVariable("Export_File")</b><br/>
          ExportText(@X.Export_File")<br/><br/>

          This example saves the path of the export file in the variable "Export_File".
          The subsequent action ExportText then writes the value to the export file. This use
          is just an example and the value could be used where ever required in a subsequent action
          and is easily accessed using smart parameters as shown.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All levels.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="SetCSVElementSeparator" qi="Ensures that all exported values are delimited by a separator designated as the parameter.">
      <p name="CustomSeparator"/>
      <ap>
        <b>CustomSeparator:</b> Uses the provided custom character as the separator between fields when
        CSV output is enabled.<br/><br/>       
        
        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <h>
        Ensures that all exported values are delimited by a separator designated as the parameter.<br/><br/>
        
        If you wish to set your own custom separator, SetCSVMode("Custom") must be called prior to exporting fields.
        If SetCSVMode("Custom") is not called, then your custom element separator will not be used for export.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          SetCSVMode("Custom")<br/>
          <b>SetCSVElementSeparator("|")</b><br/>
          This action enables CSV output using a custom separator and 
          uses "|" to delimit the Export file's values.<br/><br/>

          <b>SetCSVMode("Off")</b><br/>
          This example turns off the addition of the separator when values are
          written in the export file.<br/><br/>

          <b>SetCSVElementSeparator(" ")</b><br/>
          This example sets the separator to a space.<br/><br/>

          <b>SetCSVElementSeparator("@CHR(32)")</b><br/>
          This is an alternate example of setting the separator value
          to a space using smart parameter notation for a space.
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All levels.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        SetCSVMode<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>
    
    <method name="SetCSVMode" qi="Formats exported values to be delimited by a comma separator.">
      <p name="CSVMode"/>
      <ap>
        <b>CSVMode:</b> must be one of these values:<br/><br/>
        
        <b>ON:</b> Enables CSV formatted output using a comma separator.<br/>
        <b>CUSTOM:</b> Uses a custom separator, as set by SetElementSeparator(), between output fields.<br/>
        <b>OFF:</b> Causes no separator to be placed between output fields.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <h>
        Ensures that all exported values are delimited by a comma separator or by
        a custom separator. This output mode can be used to create a Comma Separated
        Variable, CSV, output file which is a standard way of creating rows and columns
        of data within a plain text file.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>SetCSVMode("On")</b><br/>
          ExportFieldValue("Date")<br/>
          ExportFieldValue("Number")<br/>
          ExportFieldValue("Total")<br/>
          <b>SetCSVMode("Off")</b><br/><br/>

          This sequence will export the captured values of the Date, Number, and Total Field
          objects into your Export file.  A comma will be added after each value to separate the fields.
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All levels.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        SetElementSeparator<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>
    
    
    <method name="SetExportDirectory" qi="Specifies the path to the Export file's location. Alternatively, you can use a Smart Parameter to identify a Paths.ini file that has a set of path parameters for your application - see the Parameters and Details sections below.">
      <p name="ExportDirectoryPath"/>
      <ap>
        <b>ExportDirectoryPath:</b> The complete path to the application's Export folder.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
      </ap>
      <lvl>All</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>True</b> if the path specified by the parameter exists or can be created. Otherwise, <b>False.</b>
      </ret>
      <h>
        The action's parameter specifies the path to the Export file's location.
        If the directory does not exist, the action will create the folder.<br/><br/>
        
        If it is expected that the action will create the directory, 
        the rights of the user ID running the rules must have the access to create
        the directory.  If running with Rulerunner, then it would be the ID for the
        Rulerunner service.  If it is a client running rules, then the ID running the
        client must have access.<br/><br/>

        For this action to take effect, it must be called prior to the first action that writes a
        value to the export file. <br/><br/>
        <e>
          SetExportFileName("Export_+@BATCHID+.txt")<br/>
          <b>SetExportDirectory("c:\ParentDirectory\Invoice\Export")</b><br/>
          This example uses a hard coded path to specify the output directory.<br/><br/>

          SetExportFileName("Export_+@BATCHID+.txt")<br/>
          <b>SetExportDirectory("@APPPATH(export)")</b><br/>
          In the second example, a smart parameter is used to obtain the location of the Export Directory from the
          value configured in the Application Manager.  The benefit of this approach is that the
          path is stored in a central location. If the application should be moved, such as from
          a test environment to production, then only the path in the application manager
          needs to be updated to provide the correct path.  The application rules do not need to change.<br/><br/>

          <b>SetExportDirectory("@APPPATH(export)+\+@BATCHID")</b><br/>
          This example expands on the the above by adding a unique subdirectory
          named with the ID the current batch.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="SetExportFileEncoding">
      <p name="Encoding"/>
      <ap>
        <b>Encoding:</b> The encoding to use for the exported text file. 
        The values can be "UTF8" or "UTF16".
        <br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <h>
        Determines if the Export file is created with UTF-8 or UTF-16 encoding.
        If this action is not called, then the default value is UTF-8.
        If this action is called with an in valid parameter, then the format will default to UTF-8.<br/><br/>

        For this action to take effect, it must be called prior to the first action that writes a
        value to the export file. It is recommended to call this action immediately before or immediately
        after setting the export file name and path.<br/><br/>        
        <e>
          <b>SetExportFileEncoding("UTF8")</b><br/>
          SetExportFileName("Export_+@BATCHID+.txt")<br/>
          SetExportDirectory("@APPPATH(export)")<br/>
          ExportAllFields()<br/><br/>

          This example will create files in UTF-8 format.<br/><br/>

          <b>SetExportFileEncoding("UTF16")</b><br/>
          SetExportFileName("Export_+@BATCHID+.txt")<br/>
          SetExportDirectory("@APPPATH(export)")<br/>
          ExportAllFields()<br/><br/>

          This example will create the file in UTF-16 format.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        SetExportFileName, SetExportDirectory<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="SetExportFileName" qi="Assigns a name to the current Export file.">
      <p name="ExportFileName"/>
      <ap>
        <b>ExportFileName:</b> The name and extension of the file to create and fill with the exported data.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <h>
        Assigns a name to the current Export file.  For this action to take effect, it must be called prior to the first action that writes a
        value to the export file. <br/><br/>

        If the file already exists, the file will be overwritten.<br/><br/>

        Once created, the file can be moved or sent to an external repository using subsequent Datacap actions.
        Alternatively, the export file can be saved in a directory that will be picked up by an external process.
        If an external process is picking up the exported file, it is up to the application designer to ensure that
        the external process does not try to pick up the file prior to it being completely written out to disk.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          SetExportDirectory("@APPPATH(export)")<br/>
          <b>SetExportFileName("Export_+@BATCHID+.txt")</b><br/><br/>

          This sequence establishes a series of Export files with names such as<br/>
          Export_20021231.001.txt<br/>
          Export_20021231.002.txt<br/><br/>

          SetExportDirectory("@APPPATH(export)")<br/>
          <b>SetExportFileName("@BATCHID+.txt")</b><br/><br/>
          
          This example will establish a series of Export files with Batch IDs as the name:<br/>
          20021231.001.txt<br/>
          20021231.002.txt<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All levels.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        SetExportDirectory<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="SetFillCharacter" qi="Sets the filler character to be used to expand the current value of a field in the export file.">
      <p name="FixedLengthFillCharacter"/>
      <ap>
        <b>FixedLengthFillCharacter:</b> A single character to be used as the filler value to adjust a value's length.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if more than one character is entered as a parameter or if an error occurs. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <h>
        Sets the filler character to be used to expand the current value of a field to the length
        specified by the action SetFieldLength.
        If the field value is longer than this length, it will be truncated to this length.<br/><br/>

        When using SetFillCharacter, the action SetFixedLength, ExportPageFieldFixedLengthLeftJustified 
        or ExportPageFieldFixedLengthRightJustified must also
        be used to set the maximum length of the field.  
        Use ResetFieldExportSettings to clear this setting.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          SetFixedLength("10")<br/>
          <b>SetFillCharacter("$")</b><br/><br/>
          
          This example sets the fill character to a $.  If the exported text is "Hello", then
          the value will be written to the export file as "Hello$$$$$"<br/><br/>

          SetFixedLength("10")<br/>
          SetJustifiedExportMode("R")<br/>
          <b>SetFillCharacter(" ")</b><br/><br/>

          This example uses blank spaces to pad the exported text. The padding has been set to
          right justified.  If the exported text is "Hello", the exported
          text will be preceded by 5 spaces as in "     Hello".<br/><br/>

          SetFixedLength("10")<br/>
          SetJustifiedExportMode("R")<br/>
          <b>SetFillCharacter("@CHR(32)")</b><br/><br/>
          
          This is an example similar to the previous example, but uses smart parameter
          notation to set the value to a space.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="SetFixedLength" qi="Uses the Numeric value you enter as a parameter to establish a fixed length of a value exported from the current field.">
      <p name="RequiredTextLength"/>
      <ap>
        <b>RequiredTextLength:</b> The required length of exported field data.  All data will be padded or truncated
        to this length.  If set to 0, then the length of text will not be adjusted.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
      </ap>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the parameter is not an integer or if there is an error. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <h>
        Uses the numeric value you enter as a parameter to establish a fixed length of a value exported from the current field.
        When this value is set, subsequent export actions will have their output values adjusted to be exactly the
        length specified by this action.<br/><br/>
        
        Any following actions are not required to be in the same rule as the rule that calls this action. However,
        the actions must be in the same task profile as the rule that called this action.<br/><br/>
        
        Use ResetFieldExportSettings to clear this setting or set the value to 0.<br/><br/>
        <e>          
          SetFixedLength("10")<br/>
          <b>SetFillCharacter("$")</b><br/>
          ExportText("Hello")<br/><br/>

          This example sets the fill character to a $.  If the exported text is "Hello", then
          the value will be written to the export file as "Hello$$$$$". It is not necessary
          to call this action on every object that calls an export action.  The length
          specification will stay in effect until the task profile is complete or
          until the action is called again with a new value.<br/><br/>

          SetFixedLength("10")<br/>
          SetJustifiedExportMode("R")<br/>
          <b>SetFillCharacter(" ")</b><br/>
          ExportText("Hello")<br/><br/>

          This example uses blank spaces to pad the exported text. The padding has been set to
          right justified.  If the exported text is "Hello", the exported
          text will be preceded by 5 spaces as in "     Hello".<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <see>
        ResetFieldExportSettings<br/>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    
    <method name="SetIgnoreFieldStatus" qi="Any field with this status will not be included in the export file.">
      <p name="StatusToIgnore"/>
      <ap>
        <b>StatusToIgnore:</b> An integer that represents the status of fields to be "ignored" by Export tasks so the value it contains will not be exported.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <h>
        This action establishes the status that determines if an Export task will
        export a field's value.  If the status of the field being exported matches this set value,
        the field's value will not be exported.<br/><br/>

        Any following export actions are not required to be in the same rule as the rule that calls this action. However,
        the actions must be in the same task profile as the rule that called this action.<br/><br/>
        
        Use ResetFieldExportSettings to clear this setting.<br/><br/>
        
        Any fields that have a DCO variable called "NOEXPORT" set to the value of "1" will also be
        excluded from the export file, regardless of the field's status.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>SetIgnoreFieldStatus("1")</b><br/>
          ExportCurrentField()<br/><br/>
          
          This example ensures that runtime values for fields with a "1" status will not
          be added to an Export file or update an Export database. (Typically, "1" denotes a problem field.)
          This example assumes it is running on a field level object. If the status of the field
          is "1", then the field's value will not be exported. For illustration, the example shows
          SetIgnoreFieldStatus as being called in the same ruleset as the ExportCurrentField action.
          As long as SetIgnoreFieldStatus was called prior to the export for this field, the setting
          will be in effect.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the parameter is not numeric or if there is an error. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.<br/>
        For more information about the DCO "STATUS" variable, refer to the help in the ApplicationObjects
        action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    <method name="SetJustifiedExportMode" qi="Right-justifies or left-justifies exported values.">
      <p name="JustifyMode"/>
      <ap>
        <b>JustifyMode:</b> An upper case R (right-justified) or L (left-justified) to indicate that exported
        field values should be left justified or right justified.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        Right-justifies or left-justifies a field's exported values according to the parameter you enter.
        For this setting to take affect, the action SetFixedLength must also be used to 
        set the maximum length of the field.  Left justification will add characters to the end
        of the exported text to extend it to the full length.  Right justification will add characters
        to the start of the exported text to extend it to the full length.
        If SetFixedLength is set to 0, then left and right
        justification is disabled.<br/><br/>

        Any following export actions are not required to be in the same rule as the rule that calls this action. However,
        the actions must be in the same task profile as the rule that called this action.<br/><br/>

        Use ResetFieldExportSettings to clear this setting.  If this action is not called, then it defaults to
        left-justified.<br/><br/>        
        <e>
          SetFixedLength("10")<br/>
          <b>SetJustifiedExportMode("R")</b><br/><br/>
          
          This sequence configures the export actions so that any exported text is set to 10 characters long.
          If the exported text is less than 10 characters, then it will be padded to be 10 characters long
          and because the justification is set to "Right", then padding characters will be added
          to the front of the text.
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False</b> if the parameter is not an "R" or "L", or if an error occurs. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>


    <method name="SetOMRSeparator" qi="For multi-punch OMR fields, uses the parameter's value as the separator character.">
      <p name="Separator"/>
      <ap>The separator character you want to use.</ap>
      <h>
        For multi-punch optical mark recognition, OMR, fields, uses the parameter's value as the separator character.  When
        multi-punch fields are exported, you typically do not want to use the same separator
        that you are using for CSV separators as OMR values are all within a single field.  This
        action allows you to specify a custom separator to be used when exporting OMR values.  If this
        action is not called, the default value is a space.<br/><br/>

        Any following export actions are not required to be in the same rule as the rule that calls this action. However,
        the actions must be in the same task profile as the rule that called this action.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>SetOMRSeparator(";")</b><br/><br/>
          
          This example sets the value separator to a semicolon.<br/><br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All levels.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>
    

    <method name="StartNewLine" qi="Starts a new line in your Export file.">
      <ap>None.</ap>
      <lvl>Any level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <h>
        Starts a new line in your Export file. Each call to an export action, such as ExportText or ExportCurrentValue,
        will output the value on the same line as the previous value exported.  Call StartNewLine
        to start a new output line in the export file.<br/><br/>

        Depending on the application needs, it may make sense to call this action just before the first value
        to be placed on a new line, or after the last value written to a line.<br/><br/>
        <e>
          <b>StartNewLine()</b><br/>
          ExportText("Export Output")<br/><br/>

          This sequence starts a new line and adds the text "Export Output" to the beginning of the line.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>

    
    <method name="WriteBlankLines" qi="Inserts blank lines into the Export file.">
      <p name="LineCount"/>
      <ap>
        <b>LineCount:</b> A positive integer indicating how many blank lines to add below the current line in the export file.<br/><br/>
        
        Smart parameters are supported.
      </ap>
      <h>
        Inserts <b>n</b> blank lines into the Export file.
        <e>
          <b>BlankLines("4")</b><br/><br/>

          This action inserts four empty lines, leaving the insertion point for the next output on
          the following line. Additional output will begin on the fifth line.<br/><br/>
          
          <b>BlankLines("@B.LinesToAdd")</b><br/><br/>

          This example uses the value of the batch variable "LinesToAdd" to determine how
          many lines to add to the file.  It is assumed that a previous action has
          created this batch level variable with the proper value or that the variable
          had been created in the Setup DCO in Datacap Studio and set with the number
          of lines to export.<br/>
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>All level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False,</b> if the parameter is not an integer or if an error occurs. Otherwise, <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>
        Refer to the top level help for general information about using this action library.
      </see>
    </method>
    
  </com>
</rrx>
